    "q-132164": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132164",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(-24+25) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
